"federalism | Wex | US Law | LII / Legal Information Institute (https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/federalism)\nciteturn11view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/federalism\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 48\nL0: Please help us improve our site!\nL1: \nL2: 【0†No thank you】\nL3: # federalism\nL4: \nL5: Federalism is a system of 【1†government】 in which the same territory is controlled by two levels of government. Generally, an overarching federal government is responsible for broader governance of larger territorial areas, while the smaller subdivisions, 【2†states】, and cities govern the issues of local concern. Both the federal government and the smaller political subdivisions have the power to make 【3†laws】 and both have a certain level of autonomy from each other.\nL6: In the United States, the 【4†U.S. Constitution】 has established a system of “dual 【5†sovereignty】,” under which the states have surrender many of their powers to the 【6†federal】 government, but also retain some sovereignty.\nL7: 【7†Article VI of the U.S.\nL8: Constitution】 contains the 【8†Supremacy Clause】, which reads, \"This Constitution, and the laws of the United States which shall be made in pursuance thereof; and all 【9†treaties】 made, or which shall be made, under the 【10†authority】 of the United States, shall be the supreme law of the land; and the 【11†judges】 in every state shall be bound thereby, anything in the Constitution or laws of any State to the contrary notwithstanding.\" This effectively means that when the laws of the federal government are in conflict with the laws of a state's government, the federal law will supersede the state law.\nL9: 【12†Article I, Section 8】 of the Constitution describes specific powers which belong to the federal government. These powers are referred to as 【13†enumerated powers】.\nL10: The 【14†Tenth Amendment】 reserves powers to the states, as long as those powers are not delegated to the federal government. Among other powers, this includes creating school systems, overseeing 【15†state courts】, creating public safety systems, managing 【16†business】 and trade within that state, and managing local government. These powers are referred to as \"reserved powers.\" Concurrent powers are the powers shared by both the federal government and state governments.\nL11: This includes the power to 【17†tax】, build roads, and to create 【18†lower courts】.\nL12: For more on federalism, see this 【19†Florida State University Law Review article†ir.law.fsu.edu】,  this 【20†Vanderbilt Law Review article†wp0.vanderbilt.edu】, and this 【21†Stanford Law Review article†www.stanfordlawreview.org】, the 【22†U.S. Constitution】, 【23†The Federalist No. 41†avalon.law.yale.edu】, 【24†The Federalist No. 45†avalon.law.yale.edu】, 【25†McCulloch v. Maryland, 17 U.S. 316 (1819†supreme.justia.com】)\nL13: \nL14: [Last reviewed in August of 2026 by the 【26†Wex Definitions Team†about.law.cornell.edu】]\nL15: Keywords\nL16: \nL17:   * 【27†federalism】\nL18:   * 【28†constitutional law】\nL19:   * 【29†enumerated powers】\nL20:   * 【30†reserved powers】\nL21:   * 【31†Tenth amendment】\nL22: \nL23: Wex\nL24: \nL25:   * 【32†ACADEMIC TOPICS】\nL26:   * 【33†legal history】\nL27:   * 【34†CIVICS】\nL28:   * 【35†local governmental law】\nL29:   * 【36†multi-jurisdictional law】\nL30:   * 【37†the Constitution】\nL31:   * 【38†wex articles】\nL32:   * 【39†wex definitions】\nL33:   * 【40†constitutional law】\nL34:   * 【41†government】\nL35: ## Wex Toolbox\nL36: \nL37: * * *\nL38: \nL39: * * *\nL40: \nL41:   * 【42†Accessibility】\nL42:   * 【43†About LII】\nL43:   * 【44†Contact us】\nL44:   * 【45†Advertise here】\nL45:   * 【46†Help】\nL46:   * 【47†Terms of use】\nL47:   * 【48†Privacy】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUNITARY Definition & Meaning - Merriam-Webster (https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/unitary)\nciteturn11view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Conte