ate directly in the negotiations, two of its member parties, Primero Justicia and Voluntad Popular, are represented in Figuera’s delegation. — Antonio María Delgado, Miami Herald, 9 Oct.\nL63: 2026 The unitary synthesis problem was first posed by researchers Scott Aaronson and Greg Kuperberg in 2006. — Joseph Howlett, Scientific American, 8 Oct. 2026 For many veterans of the Reagan Justice Department, though, the unitary-executive theory is an article of faith. — Margaret Talbot, New Yorker, 10 Aug. 2026 【23†See All Example Sentences for unitary】\nL64: ## Word History\nL65: \nL66: First Known Use\nL67: \nL68: circa 1790, in the meaning defined at 【24†sense 1b】\nL69: \nL70: Time Traveler\nL71: \nL72: The first known use of unitary was circa 1790\nL73: \nL74: 【25†See more words from the same year】\nL75: \nL76: ## Browse Nearby Words\nL77: \nL78: 【26†unitarity】\nL79: \nL80: unitary\nL81: \nL82: 【27†unitary color】\nL83: \nL84: 【28†See all Nearby Words】\nL85: \nL86: ## Cite this Entry\nL87: \nL88: Style\nL89: \nL90: [Select]\nL91: \nL92: “Unitary.” Merriam-Webster.com Dictionary, Merriam-Webster, https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/unitary. Accessed 1 Nov. 2026.\nL93: \nL94: 【29†Image†prod.cdn-mw.com】Copy Citation\nL95: ## Kids Definition\nL96: \nL97: unitary\nL98: \nL99: adjective\nL100: \nL101: uni·​tary 【16†ˈyü-nə-ˌter-ē 】\nL102: \nL103: 1\nL104: \nL105: a\nL106: \nL107: : of or relating to a unit\nL108: \nL109: b\nL110: \nL111: : based on or marked by unity or units\nL112: \nL113: 2\nL114: \nL115: : having the character of a unit : 【30†whole】\nL116: ## Legal Definition\nL117: \nL118: unitary\nL119: \nL120: adjective\nL121: \nL122: uni·​tary 【16†ˈyü-nə-ˌter-ē 】\nL123: \nL124: 1\nL125: \nL126: : having the character of a single thing that is a constituent of a whole\nL127: \nL128: specifically : of, relating to, or being a business with subsidiaries in other states or nations that has its state income tax figured by including the subsidiaries' income, determinin